course: everyday
lesson: 493
title: Warm parents and strict parents
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In York, Poppy-Ann comes home with 5 out of 20 in spelling and waits for trouble. Dad looks at it: «Five out of twenty? Right, chips and spellings at the kitchen table, our kid» («our kid» is Northern for my child, or my brother or sister). They turn it into a game, a chip for every word right, and by Friday she gets 16. Warm parents make mistakes feel fixable, so kids dare to try. If your home is warm, say thanks sometimes; if it isn't, you can still find warm grown-ups at school.
explain: Strict parents set firm rules, usually because they care about sleep, school and safety. In London, Daniyar's mum says homework first, no phone at dinner, bed at 8.30. His mates are on a group call at 9 and next day he's missed all the jokes. «Your mum's bare strict, bruv,» says Kwame («bare» means very). Shouting won't change the rule, but a calm deal sometimes does: «Can I stay on till 9 on Fridays?» In Galway, Siobhan's da is strict but fair: «Homework done? Grand, then yes» («grand» means fine). Strict doesn't mean always no.
explain: Every family has its own style. In Houston, Marquis's dad runs the house like the army: bed made, shoes lined up, «yes sir». At a sleepover he calls, «Lights out, gentlemen, oh-nine-hundred wake-up» (army time: 0900 is nine in the morning, 2100 is nine at night). The friend whispers, «Your dad's wild.» Marquis just laughs; he's used to it and kinda likes the order. In Bradford, Tariq's dad, strict and religious and warm underneath, says no to a sleepover party, then drives Tariq there and picks him up at nine. Strict parents often find a middle way if you ask nicely.
explain: Too few rules can be hard too. In Cornwall, Elowen's parents are lovely and relaxed, maybe too relaxed: she stays up till midnight on school nights and falls asleep in class. It's not uncool to ask for a bedtime; it helps. In Wellington, Mihi's mum is the «cool mum» who plays Fortnite with Mihi's mates; they think she's sick (which here means great). But when Mihi is sad, Mum says, «Chur, you'll be right» («chur» is Kiwi for thanks or cool, «you'll be right» means you'll be fine) and goes back to her game. Being a friend isn't the same as being a parent, and Mihi can say, «I need you to listen, not joke.»
explain: Rules make more sense when you see why. In Essex, where screens and sweets are strictly limited, Albie eats a whole bag of sweets at his mate's and is sick on the way home; now he knows why Mum's strict. In Glasgow, Kyle says «gimme the ketchup» at a mate's house, and the dad says, «Whit's the magic word, son?» («whit» is Scots for what). «Please,» says Kyle, red-faced, realising his maw (Scots for mum) was right about manners. And Ana from Lisbon, from a family of big hugs, hugs her new teacher in Leeds on day one; the teacher smiles: «We do high fives in school.» Love at home and rules at school can look different, and both are OK.
fact: Warm parents make mistakes feel fixable, so children feel safe to try again.
fact: Strict parents set firm rules, usually because they care about sleep, school and safety.
fact: Strict and fair means clear rules, explained, and a yes when you have earned it.
fact: Asking calmly for a deal, like a later bedtime at weekends, works better than shouting.
fact: Some families run on very strict routines, often from a parent's job or upbringing; there is often a lot of love inside it.
fact: Army time counts the hours from 0000 to 2359: 0900 is nine in the morning and 2100 is nine at night.
fact: Strict parents often find a middle way if you ask nicely, like going to a party but coming home early.
fact: Too few rules can be as hard as too many; children still need sleep and some rules.
fact: A parent who is fun is great, but children also need a parent who listens when they are sad.
fact: Rules often protect children from things they can't see yet, like feeling sick from too many sweets.
fact: Manners learned at home help everywhere.
fact: Families show love in different ways, and schools have their own rules about touching to keep everyone comfortable.
fact: Words in this lesson: «our kid» (North England) means my child or my brother or sister, «bare» (London) means very, «grand» (Ireland) means fine, «sick» can mean great, «chur» (NZ) means thanks or cool, «you'll be right» (NZ, Australia) means you'll be fine, «whit» means what and «maw» means mum (Scotland).
exercise: Poppy-Ann got 5 out of 20. What did Dad do? | Made it a game with chips and spellings | Shouted and took away her pocket money | Wrote a cross letter to her teacher | Said spelling doesn't matter one bit
exercise: «Right, chips and spellings, our kid.» What does «our kid» mean here? | My child | Our goat | The neighbour | Your teacher
exercise: Why do kids with warm parents often try harder? | Failing doesn't feel scary | They get paid for each try | Their homework is easier | They never make mistakes
exercise: Why does Daniyar's mum have bed at 8.30? | She cares about his sleep and school | She wants him to miss all the jokes | She doesn't like his mates on the call | She wants the house quiet for her shows
exercise: «Your mum's bare strict, bruv.» What does «bare» mean? | Very | Not | Kind of | Never
exercise: What might work better than shouting for Daniyar? | A calm deal, like Fridays till 9 | Slamming the door until she gives in | Hiding his phone under the pillow | Telling her Kwame's mum is nicer
exercise: «Homework done? Grand, then yes.» What does this show about Siobhan's da? | Strict doesn't mean always no | He always says no to everything | He forgets to check her homework | He only says yes on her birthday
exercise: «Oh-nine-hundred wake-up.» When do the boys get up? | 9 in the morning | 9 at night | 1 in the afternoon | 9 minutes past midnight
exercise: How does Marquis feel about his dad's strict routine? | He's used to it and kinda likes it | He hates it and wants to run off | He's scared of his dad every day | He's never noticed it at all, ever
exercise: Tariq's dad said no to the sleepover. What middle way did he find? | Drove him there and picked him up at nine | Let him stay at the party all weekend | Sent his big brother to go instead | Had the whole party at Tariq's house
exercise: Elowen goes to bed at midnight on school nights. What's the problem? | She's tired and falls asleep in class | Her parents are far too strict with her | She gets too much sleep for her age | Her teacher keeps her up with homework
exercise: «Your mum's sick.» What do Mihi's mates mean? | She's great | She's ill | She's mean | She's tired
exercise: When Mihi is sad, what does she need from her mum? | Listening, not jokes | More Fortnite time | A new games console | Her mates to go home
exercise: Why does Albie now know why his mum is strict about sweets? | He was sick after a whole bag | His mate told him off for it | He saw a film about teeth | He ran out of pocket money
exercise: «Whit's the magic word, son?» What is the dad asking for? | «Please» | «Abracadabra» | «Ketchup» | «Thank you, sir»
exercise: Ana hugged her teacher. What did the teacher say? | «We do high fives in school» | «Never touch me again, go away» | «Hugs are only for Portugal» | «Go and sit in the corner now»
exercise: What did Kyle learn from the ketchup moment? | His maw was right about manners | His maw was wrong about manners | Ketchup is rude in Glasgow | You never say please to dads
test: «strict parents don't love u, warm ones do». True? | No, strict rules usually come from caring | Yes, rules always mean less love | Yes, only warm parents love kids | No, warm parents love kids less
test: Daniyar: «Mum, bed at 8.30 is well unfair, everyone's on till 10». What's the cleverest next line? | «Can I stay on till 9 on Fridays?» | «I'm staying up anyway, you can't stop me» | «Kwame's mum is way nicer than you are» | «Fine, I'll never talk to you again»
test: Marquis's dad says «twenty-one hundred, lights out». What time is that? | 9 at night | 9 in the morning | 2 in the afternoon | 10 at night
test: In a cartoon, the strict dad never smiles and the kids are miserable forever. What's closer to Marquis's life? | Strict routines can have lots of love inside | Strict dads never care about their children | Children of strict dads are always unhappy | Strict houses never have any fun at all
test: Elowen: «no bedtime is sick, I'm the luckiest». What's true? | Too few rules can be hard, she's always tired | She's lucky, kids don't need any sleep at all | Bedtimes are only for babies and toddlers | Her teacher is wrong to notice she's tired
test: Mihi's mum: «chur, you'll be right». Mihi is still sad. What could she say? | «I need you to listen, not joke» | «You're the worst mum ever, go away» | «Can I play Fortnite now, then?» | «Chur» and go and cry on her own
test: What would probably happen if Albie had no rules at all about sweets? | He'd feel sick more often, and his teeth would suffer | He'd learn to love vegetables more than any of the sweets | He'd never eat a single sweet again at all | His mates would share their sweets with him
test: Tariq's dad: «no sleepover». Tariq: «can I go if I'm home by 9?» What is Tariq doing well? | Asking for a deal | Arguing loudly | Sneaking out | Giving up on it all
test: Poppy-Ann got 5 out of 20 on Monday and 16 on Friday. How many more words did she get right? | 11 | 16 | 21 | 4
test: Kyle's mate's dad: «Whit's the magic word, son?» Kyle wasn't sure what «whit» meant. What did it mean? | What | White | Wait | Wit
test: Siobhan wants a sleepover on Saturday. Da's rule: homework done means yes. It's Friday night and she has two pages left. What's her smartest plan? | Finish the two pages, then ask Da | Ask Mam, who might just say yes | Ask Da now and hope he forgets | Do one page and say it's all done
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the motives of strict parents as described in the lesson? | Concern for the child's sleep, schooling and safety | A wish to deprive the child of all enjoyment | Indifference as to the child's welfare | Obedience to a law prescribing strictness
exam: Given that a household observes army time, ascertain the hour denoted by «twenty-one hundred». | Nine o'clock in the evening | Nine o'clock in the morning | Twelve o'clock noon precisely | One o'clock in the afternoon
exam: Identify the principal difficulty arising from a household with too few rules. | Insufficient sleep and fatigue | Excessive sleep and lethargy | An excess of strict instruction | A surplus of homework assigned
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child's request to alter a household rule. | A calm proposal of terms is more effective than shouting | Shouting is the most effective means of altering a rule | Rules cannot be altered by any means whatsoever | The child must first secure the agreement of a friend
